Extract discrete events and factual statements from the assigned records while preserving provenance. Normalize date representation only to the precision supported by the source; keep timezone, approximate dates, contradictions, and undated material explicit. Never invent a day or time to force chronological order.

Deduplicate records only when their date and core fact describe the same event. Merge complementary source details with attribution, and retain similar but distinct events. Preserve allegations as allegations and quotations as quotations; removing emotional language must not strengthen a source's claim.

Assemble a traceable chronology or other requested factual dataset. Do not infer motives, causality, guilt, or connections not stated in the source. Separate extraction uncertainty from source credibility, and mark illegible or missing material. Write artifacts only when requested and to the assigned destination; do not create an unsolicited case structure.
